  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1157 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nA broad upper level trough over the Ohio Valley and high pressure at \nthe sfc will begin to build east by Wednesday, allowing for a \nsignificant warm up. Rising heights aloft with 850 mb temps of 18-19C \nwill translate to temperatures warming 10 degrees above normal on \nWednesday and Thursday with highs ranging from upper 80s to around \n90 degrees. Meanwhile, temperatures may also overachieve a bit today \nand potentially Tuesday in the low to mid 80s as model guidance \ncontinues to struggle with mixing in the boundary layer. With \ndewpoints mixing down well into the 40s each afternoon, min relative \nhumidity values will peak between 20-30%; however, light winds \nshould mitigate fire danger concerns. \n\nThe unseasonably warm conditions will not last for long as a 500 mb \nvort max and closed low ejects northeast out of the southern Plains \non Wednesday, eventually transitioning into an elongated wave by the \nend of the week. The modest increase in lift combined with a cold \nfront that will approach from the west Thursday night into Friday \nwill bring rain shower chances. The greatest risk on the new NBM now \npeaks at a 60-70% chance Friday morning into Friday afternoon. \nInstability looks to be pretty meager, supporting only a few rumbles \nof rumble at most. Rain chances then linger over the weekend as a \nshortwave associated with the northern stream quickly moves \nsoutheast towards the Great Lakes region. \n\nModel ensembles by Sunday evening support a 50-60% chance of \nexceeding 1 inch while only a 20-30% chance of exceeding 2 inches. \nGiven a steady rain that will occur in intervals over a long \nduration, the potential for flooding will be limited. The GFS has \nbeen more aggressive with QPF compared to the drier ECMWF, skewing \nthe deterministic NBM forecast too high with rainfall amounts \nexceeding 3 inches in some spots. Would expect the rainfall to \ncontinue to trend lower in the coming days as the setup is not \nfavorable for a heavy rainfall event. The increase in cloud cover \nbehind the aforementioned cold front will also translate to much \ncooler temperatures over the weekend with highs progged to only \nreach the low to mid 70s.",
